$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^{5}}{x^2}&=x^{5-2}
\\\\
&=x^3
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $\dfrac{x^m}{x^n}=x^{m-n}$. Note that the powers have the same base.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^{5}}{x^2}&=\dfrac{\overbrace{\cancel x\cdot \cancel x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x}^\text{5 times}}{\underbrace{\cancel x\cdot \cancel x}_\text{2 times}}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{x\cdot x\cdot x}_\text{3 times}
\\\\
&=x^3
\end{align}$
